'''tiar''' (tiär), ''n.'' [< F. ''tiare'', < L. ''tiara'': see ''tiara''.] A tiara. [Poetical.]

{{smaller block|Of beaming sunny rays a golden ''tiar''
Circled his head.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., iii. 625.}}}}

'''tiara''' (tī-ā′rä̤), ''n.'' [Formerly also ''tiar''; < F. ''tiare'' = Sp. Pg. It. ''tiara''; < L. ''tiara'', ''tiaras'', < Gr. τιάρα, τιάρας, τιήρης, the head-dress of the Persian kings; origin unknown.] 1. An ornament or article of dress with which the ancient Persians covered the head: a kind of turban. {{smaller|As different authors describe it it must have been of different forms. The kings of Persia alone had a right to wear it straight or erect; lords and priests wore it depressed, or turned down on the fore side. Xenophon says the tiara was encompassed with the diadem, at least in ceremonials.}}

{{smaller block|On his head . . . he ware a Persian ''tiara'', all set down with rows of so rich rubies as they were enough to speak for him that they had to judge of no mean personage.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Arcadia, v.}}}}

2. A cylindrical diadem pointed at the top, tipped with the mound and cross of sovereignty, and surrounded with three crowns, which the

readings.c2s2:
Pope wears as a symbol of his threefold sovereignty. {{smaller|Till late in the middle ages ''tiara'' was a synonym of ''mitra'', a bishop's miter, and at ceremonies of a purely spiritual character the Pope still wears the miter, not the tiara. ''Cath. Dict.''}}

{{smaller block|Gregory XI. assumed the ''tiara'' on the last day of 1370.
{{right|''The Century'', XL. 592.}}}}

'''3.''' Figuratively, the papal dignity.—'''4.''' A coronet or frontal; an ornament for the head: used loosely for any such ornament considered unusually rich: as, a ''tiara''

